{0 Testing}

A Silica app is tested at five levels. Most tests run on native OCaml, with no
browser:

{t
| Level | Tool | What it shows |
|-------|------|---------------|
| one machine | {!Silica.Machine.step} | a step gives the next state and the actions |
| every state | {!Check} | no state that the machine can reach breaks a property |
| the runtime | {!Silica_memory} | the components, timers, calls and history work together |
| the server | [Server.handle] | the HTTP policy, the handlers and the first HTML |
| the browser | jsdom and Playwright | the DOM renderer in a real page |
}

{1:machines Machines}

A machine is a value: start it, step it with events, and compare the snapshot,
the name of its state and the actions. The {{!page-tutorial}tutorial} and
{!page-machines.run} show it. This is the fastest test, and the right one for
the rules of one machine.

{1:checker The checker}

The checker runs every event in every state that it can reach, so one call
covers the paths that a list of examples would miss. Give it samples of the
server and the invariants of the app, and run it in a dune test. See
{!page-checker}.

{1:memory The memory platform}

{!Silica_memory} mounts a component with the same runtime as the browser, on
native OCaml. The test finds elements, presses them, types text, and answers
the Remote calls by hand. Nothing happens by itself: the clock moves only when
the test calls [advance].

The [Doc_hello] component calls the server when it starts. The test answers
the request:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_hello.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string = "<p>Loading.</p>"
# let request = List.hd (Silica_memory.pending page);;
val request : Silica_memory.request = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.path request, Silica_memory.body request;;
- : string * string = ("/api/greeting", "\"Ada\"")
# Silica_memory.reply request {|"Hello, Ada"|};;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string = "<p>Hello, Ada</p>"
]}

A failed call and a retry:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_hello.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.network_error (List.hd (Silica_memory.pending page)) "down";;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.press (Silica_memory.by_text page "Try again");;
- : unit = ()
# List.length (Silica_memory.requests page);;
- : int = 2
]}

Timers wait for the clock. The debounce of {!page-machines} settles 500
milliseconds after the last text:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_debounce.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# let field =
    Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Textbox ~name:"Word";;
val field : Silica_memory.element = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.input field "ocaml";;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.advance page 499;;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.text (Silica_memory.by_text page "Waiting for you to stop.");;
- : string = "Waiting for you to stop."
# Silica_memory.advance page 1;;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.html page;;
- : string =
"<div><input aria-label=\"Word\" value=\"ocaml\" /><button>Clear</button><p>You typed ocaml.</p></div>"
]}

{2:roles Roles and names}

A test finds an element as a screen reader does: by its role and its
accessible name. {!Silica_memory.by_role} gives the one element that matches,
and also takes [~level], [~checked], [~selected] and [~expanded].
{!Silica.Accessibility} computes the role and the name from the view, with the
rules of WAI-ARIA for the HTML that Silica views use; its page lists them.
Hidden elements are left out. When no element or several match, the failure
lists the elements of that role, with their names and paths, so the fix is in
the message:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount Doc_debounce.component;;
val page : unit Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Button ~name:"Clean";;
Exception:
Failure
 "Silica_memory: no button \"Clean\". The elements of role button are:\n  button \"Clear\" at div > button".
]}

{!Silica_memory.view_text} prints the view as {!Silica.View_text} does: one
line per element that has a role or a handler, with its name, its states and
its handlers. It is what a test, an agent or a reviewer reads to know what the
page shows, with no browser:

{@ocaml[
# Silica_memory.input
    (Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Textbox ~name:"Word")
    "ocaml";;
- : unit = ()
# print_string (Silica_memory.view_text page);;
textbox "Word" value="ocaml" [input]
button "Clear" [press]
text "Waiting for you to stop."
- : unit = ()
]}

{2:props A root with props}

{!Silica_memory.mount_with_props} mounts a component that has props, and
{!Silica_memory.set_props} gives it new ones, as a parent does. The type of the
handle carries the type of the props:

{@ocaml[
# let page = Silica_memory.mount_with_props ~props:"Cups" Doc_stepper.component;;
val page : string Silica_memory.t = <abstr>
# Silica_memory.press
    (Silica_memory.by_role page Silica.Accessibility.Button ~name:"More Cups");;
- : unit = ()
# Silica_memory.set_props page "Mugs";;
- : unit = ()
# print_string (Silica_memory.view_text page);;
text "Mugs: 1"
button "Fewer Mugs" [press]
button "More Mugs" [press]
- : unit = ()
]}

[mount ~answers] answers calls before the first paint, as a server render
does. {!Silica_memory.mount_app} runs a router app with a history: see
{!page-router.app}. The platform also records the actions, the running
sources, the title, the cookies, the log and the Live sockets, which the test
opens and feeds with messages.

What the memory platform does not do: it paints islands as empty elements,
it does not measure (a [Measure] gets {!Silica.Rect.zero}), and the sources
that need a layout, such as [Anchor] and [Scroll_lock], are only recorded.
Test those in a browser.

{1:server Server tests}

[Server.handle] is a function from a request to a response. With a
synchronous [Io], a test calls it directly: see {!page-server.try}. Test the
status of each check, the body of each handler, and the HTML of a page. For
Live sockets, [Server.live] runs one island with a [send] function that the
test gives, and [Server.receive] gives it a client message.

{1:browser Browser tests}

The repository has two sets of browser tests:

- [test/browser/]: Node tests over jsdom. They mount components with
  {!Silica_browser} and check the DOM, the hydration and the events. Run them
  with [dune build @test/browser/browser-test].
- [test/e2e/]: Playwright tests in Chromium, against the example server, mini
  Linear and the Silica site. They test what jsdom cannot: layout, focus, scroll,
  the history and the first paint with and without JavaScript. Run them with
  [npm --prefix test/e2e test].

A browser test waits for the attribute [data-silica-ready] on the root before
it acts: from then on the page reacts to input.
